      <h1>Capacity Testing (C20)</h1>
      <p class="muted">Battery Testing Methods · Last reviewed 2026-09-15</p>

      <div class="box">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> Capacity (Ah) is measured by discharging a fully-charged battery at a
        <strong>constant current</strong> until it reaches a cut-off voltage, then computing <strong>current × time</strong>.
        The lead-acid standard is the <strong>C20 rate</strong> — the current that drains the battery in 20 hours to
        <strong>10.5V</strong>. Capacity falls at higher rates, which is why the rate must always be stated.
      </div>

      <h2>The basic procedure</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Fully charge and let the battery rest to a stable state.</li>
        <li>Discharge at a constant current (resistive or electronic load).</li>
        <li>Stop at the cut-off voltage (10.5V for a 12V battery).</li>
        <li>Record time; capacity = discharge current × hours.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>A 60&nbsp;Ah battery discharged at 3&nbsp;A should run 20 hours. If it quits at 15 hours, its real capacity is
      45&nbsp;Ah — a 25% loss from the label.</p>

      <h2>Why the rate matters</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Discharge rate</th><th>Typical capacity delivered</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>C20 (20-hour)</td><td>Rated (100%)</td><td>Slow, extracts the electrolyte deep in the plates</td></tr>
          <tr><td>C5 (5-hour)</td><td>Lower than rated</td><td>Faster discharge wastes energy as heat</td></tr>
          <tr><td>C1 (1-hour)</td><td>Much lower</td><td>Heavy load, significant internal loss</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>Two "60&nbsp;Ah" batteries can differ in a real, faster-discharge application — which is why the rate is
      part of the spec, not an afterthought.</p>

      <h2>Reading a measured capacity</h2>
      <p>A new battery usually delivers at or above its label. Below about <strong>80% of rated C20 capacity</strong> is
      the conventional end-of-life signal for many applications. For a used battery, the measured C20 capacity is
      the honest number to compare against the label — a battery that still holds 12.6V can nonetheless have lost
      much of its capacity.</p>
      <h2>What to ask the lab</h2>
      <ul>
  <li>Which rate was the capacity measured at (C20 or C5)?</li>
  <li>What was the test temperature?</li>
  <li>Was the battery conditioned before the test?</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>The temperature correction</h2>
      <p>
        Capacity is rated at <strong>25°C</strong>; measured elsewhere, it must be corrected — cold reads lower,
        heat reads higher, and uncorrected results misstate the battery's true capacity. Standard practice applies
        the temperature-correction factors from the relevant standard (IEC 60896 / SAE) before reporting (see
        <a href="/temperature-testing/">temperature testing</a>).
      </p>



      <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
      <div class="box">
        <p><strong>I would argue:</strong> The capacity label is a promise made at one specific rate, and the most useful
        question is always "at what rate?" — because the same battery is a 60&nbsp;Ah battery at C20 and a smaller one
        at C5.</p>
        <p><strong>The reasoning:</strong> Capacity is quoted at the flattering slow rate. A buyer who understands the rate — and
        asks for the measured C20 on a used battery — knows what they are actually buying.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>This is my analysis, not a verified fact or purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

